---
import LogoExamples from '../../../../../components/templates/LogoExamples.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../../../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import {
  minimalismGuide,
  logoGallery,
  minimalistExample,
  referencePath,
} from '../../../../../content/branding-gallery';
import '../../../../../styles/branding-gallery.css';
---

<SiteLayout title="Minimalist logo design" description={minimalismGuide.description}>
  <article class="container brand-pages">
    <header class="brand-intro">
      <h1>Minimalism</h1><p class="lead">
        A minimalist logo gives people one thing to remember. A curve that feels quick. A bite taken
        from a familiar shape. The designer’s job is deciding which detail earns its place.
      </p>
    </header>
    <figure class="hero-specimen">
      <img
        src={referencePath + minimalistExample.file}
        alt="Nike’s Swoosh, a single curved mark tapering to a point"
        width="300"
        height="180"
      /><figcaption>Nike Swoosh</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <LogoExamples examples={minimalistExample.examples} />
    <section class="guide-section">
      <h2>What does it say about your brand?</h2><div>
        <p>
          A simple mark gives the eye somewhere to settle. On an architect’s drawing, precise angles
          and generous space can suggest care and accuracy. On a running shoe, a single tilted curve
          can feel fast. Both are minimalist, yet they send different signals.
        </p>
        <p>
          Decide what someone should feel first. If you want a neighbourhood bakery to feel
          welcoming, a soft curve and gently rounded lettering may do more for you than a sharply
          cut monogram.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="guide-section">
      <h2>What a designer looks for</h2><div>
        <h3>A silhouette you could draw from memory</h3><p>
          Look at the Swoosh above. The thick end grounds it, then the narrowing stroke pulls your
          eye forward. Try tracing it in the air. That change in weight is the memorable part.
        </p>
        <h3>One deliberate difference</h3><p>
          Look at Apple’s silhouette below. Cover the bite with your thumb, then uncover it. One cut
          into the edge gives an otherwise familiar fruit a distinctive outline.
        </p>
        <figure class="apple-study">
          <img
            src={referencePath + 'apple.svg'}
            width="64"
            height="76"
            alt="Apple’s silhouette with a bite cut from the right edge"
          /><figcaption>Apple</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <h3>Space that survives real use</h3><p>
          If two strokes nearly touch, they can merge when the logo shrinks. A designer adjusts
          those gaps by eye and checks them at the size of a profile picture, a receipt or a
          stitched pocket.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="guide-section">
      <h2>Where it works well</h2><div>
        <p>
          Think of a repair business using one mark on a van, a work shirt and a small invoice. A
          bold outline can carry across all three. A single-colour version also makes a rubber stamp
          or embroidered patch easier to produce.
        </p>
        <p>
          A new business may still need its name beside the symbol while customers learn the
          connection. And if every local repair firm already uses a simple spanner, taking detail
          away won’t make another spanner distinctive. Start with a feature that belongs to your
          business.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="guide-section">
      <h2>Try the small-size test</h2><div>
        <div class="size-study">
          {
            [96, 48, 24].map((size) => (
              <figure>
                <>
                  <div>
                    <img
                      src={referencePath + 'apple.svg'}
                      width={size}
                      height={size}
                      alt={`Apple mark at ${size} pixels`}
                    />
                  </div>
                  <figcaption>{size} px</figcaption>
                </>
              </figure>
            ))
          }
        </div>
        <p>
          Glance at the smallest version first. Can you still see the bite and the separate leaf? Do
          the same with your proposed logo in one colour. Then place it beside three competitors’
          marks and see whether you can pick yours out at a glance.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="next-step">
      <h2>Give your designer a starting point</h2><p>
        “We want to feel precise and approachable. Our logo needs to read on a small uniform badge
        and on a van door. The angled roofline of our workshop is something our customers already
        recognise.”
      </p><p>
        In your brief, name one feeling, one distinctive detail and the smallest place the logo will
        appear.
      </p><div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/enquiry/?service=branding">Start a branding enquiry</a><a
          href={logoGallery.route}>Compare all logo styles</a
        >
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
